<div class="callout danger" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto 20px;text-align:center;">THE DEFINING FEATURE: ASPE does NOT require consolidated financial statements as the default. A parent may elect to consolidate its subsidiaries, OR account for them using the cost method or the equity method in non-consolidated financial statements. This is a genuinely fundamental divergence from IFRS 10 and ASBE No. 33, both of which mandate consolidation.</div>



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Whether — and how — a parent reflects its subsidiaries</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1591 governs HOW a PARENT enterprise ACCOUNTS for ITS subsidiaries — and, GENUINELY distinctively, WHETHER it CONSOLIDATES them AT all. UNLIKE virtually EVERY other FRAMEWORK covered THROUGHOUT this poster LIBRARY, ASPE TREATS consolidation as an ACCOUNTING POLICY CHOICE, RATHER than a MANDATORY requirement (Section 4) — a GENUINELY fundamental, PRACTICALLY significant divergence FROM IFRS 10 and ASBE No. 33 (see the COMPANION Chinese GAAP poster EARLIER in this LIBRARY).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Defining a Subsidiary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">An entity controlled by another reporting enterprise</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO an ENTERPRISE that CONTROLS one OR more OTHER enterprises ("SUBSIDIARIES") — GOVERNING how THAT controlling RELATIONSHIP is REFLECTED (or NOT reflected, in FULL consolidation TERMS) within the PARENT'S OWN financial STATEMENTS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Definition of Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Continuing power over strategic policies, without the cooperation of others</div><div class="text-supporting">CONTROL of an ENTERPRISE is the CONTINUING power TO determine ITS strategic OPERATING, investing, AND financing POLICIES WITHOUT the CO-OPERATION of OTHERS — a DEFINITION CENTERED on POWER over KEY strategic DECISIONS, RATHER than a SPECIFIC voting-INTEREST percentage THRESHOLD alone.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>THE CENTRAL CHOICE — Three Accounting Alternatives for Subsidiaries</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Consolidation, cost method, or equity method — a genuine election</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Alternative</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Consolidation</span><span>COMBINING the PARENT'S and SUBSIDIARIES' individual FINANCIAL statements INTO a SINGLE set OF group-LEVEL statements, PER Sections 1601/1602</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Cost method</span><span>THE investment IN each SUBSIDIARY is CARRIED at COST, with INCOME recognized ONLY to the EXTENT of DIVIDENDS received FROM the subsidiary</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Equity method</span><span>THE investment IS adjusted FOR the PARENT'S share OF the subsidiary'S profit OR loss, PER the SAME mechanics USED for significant-INFLUENCE investments under SECTION 3051</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Why ASPE Permits This Choice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cost-benefit considerations for private enterprise stakeholders</div><div class="text-supporting">CONSOLIDATION is a GENUINELY complex, COSTLY exercise — ELIMINATING intragroup TRANSACTIONS, ALIGNING accounting POLICIES across the ENTIRE group, and MANAGING non-CONTROLLING interests. FOR a PRIVATE enterprise, the PRIMARY users of ITS financial STATEMENTS (LENDERS, tax AUTHORITIES, owner-MANAGERS) OFTEN have LESS need FOR a fully CONSOLIDATED, group-WIDE view than PUBLIC capital MARKETS investors WOULD. ASPE'S choice TO make CONSOLIDATION optional REFLECTS a DELIBERATE cost-BENEFIT judgment — ALLOWING private ENTERPRISES to AVOID the SUBSTANTIAL cost OF consolidation WHERE its INCREMENTAL benefit TO their OWN specific USER base is GENUINELY limited.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Consolidation — Applying Sections 1601 and 1602</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The most complete, but most costly, alternative</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE a PARENT elects CONSOLIDATION, it APPLIES Section 1601 (CONSOLIDATED Financial STATEMENTS) and SECTION 1602 (NON-controlling Interests) — COMBINING the PARENT'S and SUBSIDIARIES' financial STATEMENTS into ONE set of GROUP-level statements, ELIMINATING intragroup TRANSACTIONS and BALANCES, MUCH as WOULD be REQUIRED under IFRS 10 or ASBE No. 33.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Cost Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A simple, dividend-driven income recognition approach</div><div class="text-supporting">UNDER the COST method, the PARENT'S investment IN a SUBSIDIARY is CARRIED at ITS original COST, WITH investment INCOME recognized ONLY to the EXTENT of DIVIDENDS received (OR receivable) FROM the SUBSIDIARY — a GENUINELY simple, LOW-cost alternative, THOUGH one that DOES NOT reflect the SUBSIDIARY'S own UNDERLYING profitability WITHIN the PARENT'S own FINANCIAL statements.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>The Equity Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A middle ground between cost and full consolidation</div><div class="text-supporting">UNDER the EQUITY method, the PARENT'S investment IS adjusted TO reflect ITS share OF the SUBSIDIARY'S profit OR loss (and OTHER equity CHANGES) EACH period — REFLECTING the SUBSIDIARY'S underlying PERFORMANCE within a SINGLE investment LINE, WITHOUT the FULL line-by-LINE combination CONSOLIDATION requires.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Consistency of Method Across Subsidiaries</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Applying the same policy uniformly</div><div class="text-supporting">AN enterprise APPLIES the SAME accounting METHOD to ALL of ITS subsidiaries CONSISTENTLY — CHOOSING, FOR example, to CONSOLIDATE all SUBSIDIARIES, or to APPLY the COST or EQUITY method TO all of THEM, RATHER than MIXING approaches ARBITRARILY across DIFFERENT subsidiaries WITHIN the SAME group.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — Electing the Cost Method Instead of Consolidation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A dramatically simpler presentation</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A private holding company's non-consolidated statements</div><div class="wk-body">A private holding company owns 100% of an operating subsidiary, originally acquired for $2,000,000. During the year, the subsidiary earns $500,000 of net income and pays the parent a $150,000 dividend.</div><div class="wk-answer">ELECTING the COST method, the PARENT'S own FINANCIAL statements SHOW the INVESTMENT at ITS ORIGINAL $2,000,000 COST (UNCHANGED), with INVESTMENT income OF <strong>$150,000</strong> (the DIVIDEND received) — the SUBSIDIARY'S OWN $500,000 net INCOME is NOT reflected AT all WITHIN the PARENT'S non-CONSOLIDATED statements.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example — Applying the Equity Method to a Subsidiary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Reflecting the subsidiary's underlying performance within a single line</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: The same facts, using the equity method instead</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, the parent instead elects the equity method for its 100%-owned subsidiary.</div><div class="wk-answer">The PARENT recognizes EQUITY income OF $500,000 (its FULL share of the SUBSIDIARY'S net INCOME), INCREASING the INVESTMENT'S carrying AMOUNT to $2,000,000 + $500,000 − $150,000 (DIVIDENDS received, WHICH reduce the INVESTMENT balance UNDER the equity METHOD) = <strong>$2,350,000</strong> — a MEANINGFULLY different OUTCOME than the COST method PRODUCED for the SAME underlying FACTS.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1591 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy elected (consolidation, cost method, or equity method) and applied consistently</span><span>Nature of the parent's relationship with each significant subsidiary</span><span>For non-consolidated presentations, the fact that the statements are non-consolidated, and why</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether control genuinely exists over a particular investee, triggering subsidiary classification</span><span>Which of the three accounting alternatives best serves the enterprise's users, given cost-benefit considerations</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal beyond those inherent in the chosen method's own mechanics (e.g. impairment testing of the investment under the cost or equity method)</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 10 and ASBE No. 33</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1591 vs IFRS 10 / ASBE No. 33</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1591</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 10 / ASBE No. 33</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Consolidation requirement</span><span>OPTIONAL — a genuine policy choice</span><span>MANDATORY for all controlled subsidiaries</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Definition of control</span><span>Continuing power over strategic policies</span><span>Power, exposure to variable returns, and ability to use power to affect returns</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">THIS is the SINGLE most SIGNIFICANT structural DIVERGENCE this ENTIRE ASPE poster series HAS yet UNCOVERED relative TO IFRS and ASBE — CONSOLIDATION being OPTIONAL, rather THAN mandatory, REPRESENTS a GENUINELY fundamental DEPARTURE from the GROUP-reporting philosophy UNDERPINNING virtually EVERY other MAJOR accounting FRAMEWORK covered THROUGHOUT this LIBRARY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every subsidiary accounting choice lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cost method dividend income</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Investment unchanged; income limited to dividends received</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Equity method income share</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Investment balance adjusted for the parent's share of subsidiary profit/loss</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1582</span><span>Business Combinations — governs the initial acquisition accounting this Section's subsequent choice of method applies to</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3051</span><span>Investments — supplies the equity method mechanics this Section applies to subsidiary accounting</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Non-consolidated financial statements</div><div class="glossary-def">Financial statements presenting a parent's own investment in subsidiaries (via the cost or equity method) rather than combining subsidiaries' assets, liabilities, and results line-by-line.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Cost method</div><div class="glossary-def">An accounting method carrying an investment in a subsidiary at its original cost, recognizing income only to the extent of dividends received.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe1591 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
